  "all_text": "S. Yao, S. Xie and T. Li Automatica 174 (2025) 112116 choosing a diminishing step size. Moreover, Zhang, Ravier, Za- vlanos, and Tarokh ( December, 2019 ) proposed an online gradient tracking algorithm with a balanced graph and demonstrated that dynamic regret can be sublinear, and Akbari, Gharesifard, and Linder ( 2015 ) proposed an online distributed subgradient push- sum algorithm with an unbalanced graph and showed that the upper bound of the static regret is sublinear when choosing a specific diminishing step size, but its dynamic regret cannot achieve sublinear property while choosing a constant or diminishing step size. Furthermore, Zhang, Shi, Zhang, and Yuan ( 2023 ) proposed an online distributed Frank–Wolfe algorithm with a balanced graph, and proved that the upper bound of the dynamic regret is sublinear when the global loss function is convex, smooth and Lipschitz, where the step size is related to the loss variance. The upper bound of the dynamic regret is O ( T (1 H T ) D T ), where H T T t 1 max i max x f i t 1 ( x ) f i t ( x ) , D T T t 1 max i max x f i t 1 ( x ) f i t ( x ) . Also, Shahrampour and Jadbabaie ( 2017 ) proposed an online distributed mirror descent algorithm with a balanced graph, and proved that its dynamic regret is sublinear when the loss function is convex and Lipschitz. The upper bound of the dynamic regret is O ( TC T (1 2 ( W ))), where 2 ( W ) is the second largest singular of matrix W and C T T t 1 x t 1 Ax t . Here, matrix A is known in the network, and x t T t 1 is the minimizer sequence. Moreover, Yang and Chen ( 2020 ) proposed an online distributed projection subgradient algorithm under an unbalanced graph and proved that the upper bound of the dynamic regret is O ( S T T 1 a 2 ) with adequate step sizes when the loss function is strongly convex and the constraint set is closed, convex and bounded, where S T T 1 t 1 x t 1 x t , and a (0 5 1). To sum up, most of the existing literature on the online distributed optimization problems are constrained by the fol- lowing aspects. (i) Some works only consider the static regret analysis of the proposed algorithms, which cannot reflect the algorithms’ capability of tracking global dynamic optimal solu- tions. (ii) The proposed online distributed algorithms lack the ability to learn continually, which means that their dynamic regret cannot be sublinear. (iii) Most works focus on the case where the communication network is balanced. In order to overcome these constraints, this paper considers an online distributed optimization problem under an unbalanced graph. Specifically, the proposed algorithm first conducts a step of weighted average, and then exploits a fixed step size to as- sure that the algorithm can learn continually. Note that Zhang et al. ( December, 2019 ) considered an online distributed algo- rithm with no constraints, where the communication network is balanced and the loss function is strongly convex and smooth, and it demonstrated that the proposed algorithm can achieve sublinear dynamic regret while step size is properly chosen. The proposed algorithm in this paper fits more practical optimization problems compared with Zhang et al. ( December, 2019 ), Yan, Sundaram, Vishwanathan, and Qi ( 2012 ), Zhang et al. ( 2023 ), and Shahrampour and Jadbabaie ( 2017 ). This paper refers to the convergence analysis method in Pu, Shi, Xu, and Nedić ( 2020 ), and proves that the algorithm we proposed can achieve sublinear dynamic regret. In recent years, researchers made much effort on proposing offline Push–Pull based distributed optimization algorithms. Pu ( December, 2020 ) considered an offline robust Push–Pull algorithm, which can converge to a neighborhood of the optimal solution in expecta- tion, and Xiong, Wu, You, and Xie ( 2022 ) proposed the Q-DGT algorithm, which can handle the situation that the communica- tion bandwidth is limited. Since this paper considers the online convex optimization problem, higher demand on matrix analysis will be imposed on analyzing the dynamic regret of the pro- posed online distributed algorithm. The main contributions of this paper can be summarized as follows. (i) This paper proposes a novel online distributed optimization algorithm that can learn the optimal solution continually. The innovation of our proposed algorithm includes the following two aspects. We introduce the gradient value of the local loss function into the algorithm when time horizon are t and t 1 to ensure that the nodes can track the dynamic optimal solution. Besides, we introduce a scaling factor and choose adequate step sizes, and the dynamic regret of our proposed algorithm can be sublinear under comparatively weak assumptions, i.e., the loss function is convex, smooth, and Lipschitz. (ii) This paper proves that the dynamic regret of the proposed algorithm can be controlled by two terms which are relevant to the variation of the loss function under adequate assumptions, and the upper bound of the dynamic regret is O ( T (1 P T ) V T ) if the loss function is strongly convex and smooth, and the upper bound is O ( T P T ) if the loss function is convex, smooth and Lipschitz. The definitions of P T and V T are introduced in Section 3 . (iii) Compared with those online dis- tributed optimization algorithms under a balanced graph ( Fosson , 2020 ; Hosseini et al. , 2013 ; Shahrampour & Jadbabaie , 2017 ), the matrix theory and properties of unbalanced graphs are needed to further analyze the dynamic regret of our proposed algorithm. Specifically, ‘‘unbalanced’’ refers to matrices R and C , which are row stochastic and column stochastic respectively in this paper, and they are not doubly stochastic. The unbalancedness is manifested by u and , which are the left eigenvector of the matrix R and the right eigenvector of the matrix C , respectively. We use matrix theory to eliminate the difficulties brought by u and in the regret analysis, which increases difficulty of our analysis. The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. In Sec- tion 2 , we formulate the problem and give the assumptions needed for analyzing the dynamic regret. We propose an online distributed Push–Pull algorithm and provide results of dynamic regret analysis in Section 3 , and we present the proofs of the main results in Section 4 , respectively. At last, two numerical experiments are used to verify the correctness of our results in Section 5 , and conclusions are presented in Section 6 . Notations : denotes the 2-norm of a matrix, F denotes the F -norm of a matrix, A denotes the transpose of the matrix A , n denotes the n -dimensional Euclidean space, m n denotes the m n -dimensional Euclidean space, I denotes the n -identity matrix, 1 denotes a n -dimensional vector with all ones, and M n denotes the set of all n n matrix. Also, by b n O ( r n ), we mean that lim sup n b n r n , where { b n n 0} is a real number sequence, and { r n n 0} is a positive number sequence. Moreover, ( ) denotes the projection on constraint set . 2. Problem formulation Consider a communication network with n nodes, every node has the capability of communication, calculation, and storing. The online distributed optimization problem can be expressed as follows: min x f t ( x ) n i 1 f i t ( x ) t 0 1 T (1) where f i t ( ) is the loss function of node i at time instant t . Let x t arg min x f t ( x ) be the optimal value at time instant t . We use a tuple ( ) to describe the network structure, where 1 2 n is the set of nodes, and is the set of edges. Thus, ( i j ) if and only if the i th agent can send message directly to the j th agent. We define W ij n n n n as a weight matrix, where ( i j ) if and only if ij 0. Given 2",
